    50	
    51	
    52	
    53	
    54	A hypergraph consists of a set of vertices together with a set of edges.  While edges in graphs always
    55	have exactly two vertices, hypergraphs may have edges of any cardinality, finite or infinite.  Some authors
    56	exclude edges that are empty of have a single vertex, but for this article, edges may be of any size.
    57	After considering alternatives for the representations of edges, we will turn to results on the strength of
    58	theorems related to three types of vertex colorings.
    59	
    60	
    61	\subsection*{Representations of edges}
    62	
    63	The edges of hypergraphs can be presented in a variety of ways.  If all the edges are finite,
    64	each edge can be encoded by a single number.  In this case, the edges can be represented
    65	by a set of codes or by a sequence of codes.  Finite edges and infinite edges can both be
    66	represented by a sequence of characteristic functions.  Some changes in representation can be
    67	carried out in $\rca$, while others require additional set comprehension, as shown by the next
    68	four results.
    69	
    70	\begin{thm}\label{rep1}
    71	$(\rca )$ If $H$ is a hypergraph with finite edges represented by a set of edges, then there is a hypergraph $H'$ with exactly the same edges represented by a sequence of its edges, possibly with repetitions.	
    72	\end{thm}
    73	
    74	\begin{proof}
    75	We argue in $\rca$. Suppose $H=\left<V,E\right>$ is a hypergraph with finite edges, where $E$ is a set of integer codes for the edges of $H$. Let $e_0$ be an integer code for an edge of $H$. Define the function $f:\mathbb{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{N}$ by
    76	$$f(n) =  \begin{cases} n & n\in E \\ e_0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}$$
    77	Then the range of $f$ is $E$, and $V$ together with $f$ represents $H$ using a sequence of codes.
    78	\end{proof}
    79	
    80	
    81	\begin{thm}\label{rep2}
    82	$(\rca)$ If $H$ is a hypergraph with finite edges represented by a sequence of its edges, then there is a hypergraph $H'$ with exactly the same edges represented by a sequence of characteristic functions for its edges.
    83	\end{thm}
    84	
    85	\begin{proof}
    86	We argue in $\rca$. Suppose $H=\left<V,E\right>$ is a hypergraph with finite edges, where $E$ is the sequences for the edges of $H$. Define the characteristic function $\chi_i$ by
    87	$$\chi_i(v_j) =  \begin{cases} 1 & v_j\in \left<e_i\right> \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}$$
    88	By recursive comprehension, the sequence of characteristic function $\left<\chi_i\right>_{i\in\mathbb{N}}$ exists. The vertices $V$ together with the sequence of characteristic functions for each edge represent $H$.
    89	\end{proof}
    90	
    91	
    92	\begin{thm}\label{rep3}
    93	$(\rca )$  The following are equivalent:
    94	\begin{enumerate}
    95	\item $\aca$. \label{rep31}
    96	\item If $H$ is a hypergraph with finite edges represented by a sequence of edges, then $H$ can be represented by a set of edges.\label{rep32}
    97	\end{enumerate}
    98	\end{thm}
    99	
   100	\begin{proof}
   101	First we will prove that (\ref{rep31}) implies (\ref{rep32}). Reasoning in $\aca$, let $H$ be a hypergraph with finite edges represented by the sequence $\left<e_i\right>_{i\in\mathbb{N}}$. Arithmetical comprehension can prove the edge set $\{e\mid\exists i(e=e_i)\}$ exists.
   102		
   103	To prove the converse, by Lemma III.1.3 of Simpson \cite{simpson}, it suffices to use item (\ref{rep32}) to prove the existence of the range of an injection. Let $g:\mathbb{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{N}$ be an injection. Define the hypergraph $H=\left<V,E\right>$ as follows. Let $\mathbb{N}$ be the set of vertices. Define a sequence of edges $\left<e_n\right>_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ by $e_n=\{0,g(n)+1\}$.
   104	Note that $m$ is in the range of $g$ if and only if the set $\{0,m+1\}$ is an edge of $H$. Given the set of edges of $H$, recursive comprehension proves the existence of the range of $g$.	
   105	\end{proof}
   106	
   107	
   108	\begin{thm}\label{rep4}
   109	$(\rca )$ The following are equivalent:
   110	\begin{enumerate}
   111	\item $\aca$. \label{rep41}
   112	\item If $H$ is a hypergraph with finite edges represented by a sequence of characteristic functions, then $H$ can be represented by a sequence of finite set codes for edges.\label{rep42}
   113	\end{enumerate}
   114	\end{thm}
   115	
   116	\begin{proof}
   117	We begin by proving that \ref{rep41} implies \ref{rep42}. Reasoning in $\aca$, let $H$ be a hypergraph with finite edges represented by the sequence of characteristic functions $\left<e_i\right>_{i\in\mathbb{N}}$. Define $s_i=\{j\mid e_i(j)=1\}$ for $i\in\mathbb{N}$. Then the sequence $\left<s_i\right>_{i\in\mathbb{N}}$ is arithmetically definable.
   118		
   119	To prove the converse, it suffices to use statement \ref{rep42} to prove the existence of a range of an injection. Let $g:\mathbb{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{N}$ be that injection. Let $H$ be the hypergraph with vertex set $\mathbb{N}$ and edges defined by the sequence of characteristic functions $\left<e_i\right>_{i\in\mathbb{N}}$ defined as follows	
   120	$$e_i(n) = \begin{cases} 1 & n=2i \vee n=2i+2 \\ 0 & n=2j\wedge j\not\in \{i,i+1\} \\ 1 & n=2j+1\wedge g(j)=i \\ 0 & n=2j+1\wedge g(j)\neq i \end{cases}$$
   121	The recursive comprehension axiom proves the existence of $\left<e_i\right>$ using $g$ as a parameter. Applying the principle of statement 2, let $\left<s_i\right>_{i\in\mathbb{N}}$ be a sequence of finite set codes for the edges of $H$. Given any value $y$, successively examine the set codes until we locate the unique $s_i$ encoding a set containing $2y$ and $2y+2$. If this set contains only $2y$ and $2y+2$, then $y$ is not in the range of $g$, but if the set also contains another element, then $y$ is in the range of $g$. Thus, recursive comprehension proves the existence of the range of $g$. 
   122	\end{proof}
   123	
   124	
   125	
   126	In order to state compactness results for vertex colorings, we need to consider finite substructures
   127	of hypergraphs.
   128	The following terminology is based on that of Berge \cite{berge}.  When constructing substructures
   129	of a hypergraph $H$, one can either require that all edges in the substructure are edges of $H$, or allow
   130	edges of the substructure to be subsets of edges of $H$.  
   131	
   132	\begin{defn}
   133	A {\sl partial hypergraph} of a hypergraph $H$ consists of a subset $V$ of the vertices
   134	of $H$ and a subset $E$ of the edges of $H$ such that for every edge $e \in E$, $e \subset V$.
   135	\end{defn}
   136	
   137	\begin{defn}
   138	A {\sl partial subhypergraph} of a hypergraph $H$ consists of a subset $V$ of the vertices of $H$ and a set $E$ of
   139	subsets of $V$ such that for every $e\in E$, there is an edge $e^\prime \in H$ such that $e = e^\prime \cap V$.
   140	\end{defn}
   141	
   142	\subsection*{Vertex colorings and finite edges}
   143	
   144	A vertex coloring is a map from the vertices of a hypergraph into a set of colors, usually coded
   145	by a subset of $\nat$.
   146	In the literature, most definitions for vertex colorings are stated for finite hypergraphs,
   147	but those listed below extend naturally to
   148	infinite hypergraphs.  The definition for
   149	conflict-free colorings is based on that of Smorodinsky \cite{smo}.  The other definitions can be found in the book of Berge \cite{berge}.
   150	
   151	\begin{defn}  Suppose $\hypg{V}{E}$ is a hypergraph.  Let $\chi : V \to \nat$ be a coloring of the vertices.  We say:
   152	\begin{enumerate}
   153	\item  $\chi$ is a $k$-coloring if the range of $\chi$ is contained in $\{0, 1, \dots k-1\}$.
   154	\item  $\chi$ is {\sl proper} if $\chi$ is non-constant on each edge that contains more than one vertex.
   155	\item  $\chi$ is {\sl strong} if $\chi$ is injective on each edge.
   156	\item $\chi$ is {\sl conflict-free} \cite{smo} if each edge contains one vertex whose color matches no other vertex in the edge.
   157	That is, $\forall e \exists j ~ | \{ v \in e \mid \chi (v) = j \}| = 1$.
   158	\end{enumerate}
   159	\end{defn}
   160	
   161	Theorem 3.4 of Hirst \cite{hirstmt} states that $\wkl$ is equivalent to the statement that every
   162	locally $k$-colorable graph has a $k$-coloring.  The following result generalizations this theorem
   163	to the hypergraph setting, for hypergraphs with sequences or sets of finite edges.
   164	
   165	
   166	\begin{thm}\label{pcffA}%strong finite colorings, result A
   167	$(\rca)$  For $k \ge 2$, the following are equivalent:
   168	\begin{enumerate}
   169	\item $\wkl$.\label{pcffA1}
   170	\item Let $H$ be a hypergraph with a sequence of finite edges.  If every finite
   171	partial hypergraph of $H$ has a proper $k$-coloring, then $H$ has a proper $k$-coloring.\label{pcffA2}
   172	\item Statement {\rm(\ref{pcffA2})} with ``proper'' replaced by ``conflict-free''.\label{pcffA3}
   173	\item Statement {\rm(\ref{pcffA2})} with ``sequence of edges'' replaced by ``set of edges.''\label{pcffA4}
   174	\item Statement {\rm(\ref{pcffA3})} with ``sequence of edges'' replaced by ``set of edges.''\label{pcffA5}
   175	\end{enumerate}
   176	\end{thm}
   177	
   178	\begin{proof}
   179	To prove that (\ref{pcffA1}) implies (\ref{pcffA2}), assume $\wkl$ and let $H$ be a hypergraph with vertex set $\{v_1, v_2, \ldots\}$ such that every finite partial hypergraph of $H$ has a proper $k$-coloring.  For every $n$, let $m_n$ be the least integer such that $m_n > m_{n-1}$ and if a vertex $v_i$ appears in one of the first $n$ edges of $H$ then $i \le m_n$.  Let $H_n$ be the finite partial hypergraph with vertex set $\{v_1, v_2, \ldots, v_{m_n}\}$ and the first $n$ edges of $H$.  Let $T$ be the tree consisting of those finite sequences $\sigma$ in $k^{<\nat}$ such that whenever length$(\sigma)=m_n$, $\sigma$ is a proper $k$-coloring of $H_n$.  Since every finite partial hypergraph of $H$ has a proper $k$-coloring, $T$ must contain infinitely many sequences.  By $\wkl$, $T$ has an infinite path.  This path yields a proper $k$-coloring of $H$.
   180	
   181	To prove that (\ref{pcffA1}) implies (\ref{pcffA3}), repeat the previous proof replacing every instance of the word ``proper'' by ``conflict-free.''  Theorem \ref{rep1} shows that (\ref{pcffA2}) implies (\ref{pcffA4}) and (\ref{pcffA3}) implies (\ref{pcffA5})
   182	It remains to show that both (\ref{pcffA4}) and (\ref{pcffA5}) imply (\ref{pcffA1}).  For graphs, that is, for hypergraphs in which every edge
   183	has exactly two vertices, every coloring is proper if and only if it is conflict-free.  Thus, both (\ref{pcffA4}) and (\ref{pcffA5}) imply that if every finite
   184	subgraph of a graph $H$ is $k$-colorable, then $H$ can be $k$-colored.  This implies $\wkl$ by Theorem 3.4 of Hirst \cite{hirstmt}.
   185	\end{proof}
   186	
   187	For the analog of the previous theorem for 
   188	graphs with edges represented by sequences of characteristic functions, additional set comprehension strength is required.
   189	
   190	\begin{thm}\label{conj2}
   191	$(\rca )$  For any $k\ge 2$, the following are equivalent.
   192	\begin{enumerate}
   193	\item \label{conj21} $\aca$.
   194	\item \label{conj22} Suppose $H$ is a hypergraph with finite edges given by a sequence of characteristic functions.  If every finite
   195	partial hypergraph of $H$ has a proper $k$-coloring then $H$ has a proper $k$-coloring.
   196	\item \label{conj23} Statement {\rm(\ref{conj22})} with ``proper'' replaced by ``conflict-free.''
   197	\end{enumerate}
   198	\end{thm}
   199	
   200	\begin{proof}
   201	To prove that (\ref{conj21}) implies (\ref{conj22}), fix $k$, assume $\aca$, and suppose $H$ is a hypergraph with finite edges
   202	given by a sequence of characteristic functions.  Further suppose that every finite partial hypergraph of $H$ can be properly $k$-colored.
   203	By Theorem \ref{rep4}, we can find the sequence of edges for $H$.  By Theorem (\ref{pcffA}) and the fact that $\aca$ implies $\wkl$,
   204	$H$ has a proper $k$-coloring.
   205	
   206	The proof that (\ref{conj21}) implies (\ref{conj23}) is similar to the preceding argument, with proper replaced by conflict-free.
   207	
   208	For the reversals, we begin by considering the proof that (\ref{conj22}) implies (\ref{conj21}) for $k=2$.  By Lemma III.1.3 of Simpson
   209	\cite{simpson}, it suffices to use $\rca$ and (\ref{conj22}) to prove the existence of the range of an arbitrary injection $f$.  The vertices
   210	of our hypergraph $H$ will be $\{ b_ i \mid i \in \nat \} \cup \{ v_{i,j} \mid i,j \in \nat \}$.  The edges of $H$ will be represented by
   211	characteristic functions for the following sets.  For all $n$ and $i$, $p_{n,i} = \{ v_{n,i} , v_{n,i+1} \}$,
   212	$q_n = \{ b_n , b_{n+1} \}$,
   213	$r_n = \{ b_{2n} , v_{f(n), 2n} \}$, and
   214	$s_n =\{ v_{n,0}, b_{2n+1} \} \cup \{ b_{2i} \mid f(i) = n\}$.
   215	Because $f$ is an injection, $b_{2i}$ is in $s_n$ if and only if $\exists t< 2i (f(t) = n )$, so the characteristic function
   216	for $s_n$ is uniformly computable from $f$.  Via dovetailing, recursive comprehension suffices to prove the existence
   217	of a sequence of characteristic functions for all these edges of $H$.
   218	Note that edges of the form $s_n$ contain two vertices if $n$ is not in the range of $f$ and three vertices if $n$ is in the range.
   219	All other edges contain exactly two vertices.
   220	
   221	We claim that every finite partial subhypergraph of $H$ has a proper 2-coloring.  Let $G$ be a partial subhypergraph.
   222	Let $n_0$ be the maximum natural number such that $b_{2n_0}$ is in $G$. Define the coloring $\chi$ by
   223	$\chi (b_n ) = n \mod 2$ if $n\le 2n_0 + 1$, $\chi (v_{n,j} )= j \mod 2$ if $\forall t \le n_0 (f(t) \neq n)$,
   224	and $\chi (v_{n,j} ) = j+1 \mod 2$ if $\exists t \le n_0 ~(f(t) = n)$.  The first clause guarantees that $\chi$ is a proper
   225	coloring with regard to every edge of the form $q_n$, and the last two clauses insure that $\chi$ is proper on edges of
   226	the forms $p_{n,i}$, $r_n$ and $s_n$ restricted to the vertices of $G$.
   227	
   228	Apply (\ref{conj22}) to obtain a proper 2-coloring of $H$.  If necessary, permute the colors so that $\chi( b_0 ) = 0$.
   229	Fix $n$.  If $f(t) = n$ for some $t$, then $r_t = \{ b_{2t} , v_{n,2t} \}$ is in $H$, so $\chi (v_{n,0} ) = 1$.  If $n$ is not
   230	in the range of $f$, then $s_n = \{v_{n,0}, b_{2n+1}\}$ is in $H$, so $\chi (v_{n,0} ) = 0$.  Thus
   231	the range of $f$ is defined by $\{ n \mid \chi (v_{n,0} ) = 1\}$, which exists by recursive comprehension.
   232	
   233	The proof that (\ref{conj22}) implies (\ref{conj21}) can be extended to values of $k$ greater than $2$ by adding
   234	a complete graph on $k-2$ vertices to $H$ and connecting each vertex in the complete graph to every vertex in
   235	the prior construction.  Finally, for graphs with edges of size at most 3, any coloring is conflict-free if and only if
   236	it is proper.  Thus (\ref{conj23}) implies (\ref{conj21}) can be proved by replacing all uses of ``proper'' in the
   237	preceding arguments with ``conflict-free.''
   238	\end{proof}
   239	
   240	For strong colorings, edge representation does not affect the strength of the coloring statements.
   241	Additionally, in some settings, finite colorability suffices to imply $\wkl$ over $\rca$.  This provides an interesting
   242	contrast to the case for graphs, as described following the proof of the next result.
   243	
   244	\begin{thm}\label{sfcA}%strong finite colorings, result A
   245	$(\rca)$  The following are equivalent:
   246	\begin{enumerate}
   247	\item $\wkl$.\label{sfcA1}
   248	\item Let $H$ be a hypergraph with any edge representation.  If there is a $k$ such that every finite
   249	partial hypergraph of $H$ has a strong $k$-coloring, then $H$ has a strong $k$-coloring.\label{sfcA2}
   250	\item Let $H$ be a hypergraph with a set of finite sets for edges.  If every finite partial hypergraph
   251	of $H$ has a strong $3$-coloring, then $H$ has a strong $k$-coloring for some $k$.\label{sfcA3}
   252	\item Let $H$ be a hypergraph with a sequence of finite sets for edges.  If every finite partial hypergraph
   253	of $H$ has a strong $2$-coloring, then $H$ has a strong $k$-coloring for some $k$.\label{sfcA4}
   254	\end{enumerate}
   255	\end{thm}
   256	
   257	\begin{proof}
   258	To prove that (\ref{sfcA1}) implies (\ref{sfcA2}), assume $\wkl$
   259	and let $H$ be a hypergraph.  By Theorem \ref{rep1} and Theorem \ref{rep2},
   260	we may assume that the edges of $H$ are given by a sequence of characteristic functions.
   261	Fix $k$ and suppose that for every $n$, the partial subhypergraph given by the first $n$ values of each of the
   262	the first $n$ characteristic functions has a strong $k$-coloring.  Let $T$ be the tree consisting of those finite
   263	sequences $\sigma$ in $k^{<\nat}$ such that $\sigma$ is a strong $k$-coloring of the partial subhypergraph
   264	defined by the first $\lh (\sigma )$ many values of the first $\lh (\sigma )$ many edge characteristic functions.
   265	Because the finite partial hypergraphs of $H$ are colorable, $T$ must contain infinitely many sequences.
   266	By $\wkl$, $T$ has an infinite path.  This path yields a strong $k$-coloring of $H$.
   267	
   268	Note that (\ref{sfcA3}) follows immediately from from (\ref{sfcA2}) restricted to $k=3$.  To prove that (\ref{sfcA3}) implies
   269	(\ref{sfcA1}), by Lemma IV.4.4 of Simpson \cite{simpson}, it suffices to use (\ref{sfcA3}) to separate the ranges
   270	of injections with disjoint ranges.  Fix injections $f$ and $g$ such that $\forall m \forall n (f(m) \neq g(n))$ and
   271	construct a hypergraph $H$ as follows.
   272	The vertices of $H$ consist of the sets $U=\{u _i \mid i \in \nat \}$ and $V=\{v_i \mid i \in \nat\}$.
   273	For each triple $i$, $j$, and $k$, the edge $\{ u_i , u_j , v_k \}$ is in $H$ if and only if $i<k$, $j<k$,
   274	$\exists t<k ~f(t)=i$, and $\exists t<k ~g(t) = j$.  Note that each edge contains exactly three vertices and
   275	exactly one vertex from $V$.  No edge contains two vertices from $U$ indexed by elements of the range of $f$, and
   276	similarly, pairs of vertices from $U$ indexed by elements of the range of $g$ are forbidden within an edge.
   277	Let $H_0$ be a finite partial hypergraph consisting of vertices $\{u _{i_0}, \dots u_{i_m} \}$ and
   278	$\{v_{j_0} , \dots v_{j_n} \}$, and some or all of the edges of $H$ with vertices entirely contained in these sets.
   279	The coloring that assigns color $0$ to all vertices $u_{i_k}$ such that
   280	$\exists t<j_n ~ f(t) = i_k $, color $1$ to all vertices $u_{i_k}$ such that
   281	$\exists t < j_n ~ g(t) = i_k$, and color $3$ to all remaining vertices is a strong $3$-coloring of $H_0$.
   282	Thus, every finite partial hypergraph of $H$ has a strong $3$-coloring.  Apply (\ref{sfcA3}) to obtain a
   283	$k$-coloring of $H$.  Note that if $\exists t ~f(t) = i$ and $\exists t ~g(t) = j$, then $u_{i}$ and $u_{j}$
   284	are included in an edge, and so $u_{i}$ and $u_{j}$ must have distinct colors.  By bounded $\Sigma^0_1$
   285	comprehension (which is provable in $\rca$ by Remark II.3.11 of Simpson \cite{simpson}) the set
   286	$C = \{ i < k \mid \exists t ~\chi (u_{f(t)} ) = i \}$ exists.  $\rca$ proves that the set $\{ j \in \nat \mid \chi (u_j ) \in C \}$ exists,
   287	contains the range of $f$, and is disjoint from the range of $g$.
   288	
   289	Item (\ref{sfcA4}) also follows immediately from (\ref{sfcA2}).  As in the previous paragraph, to prove that
   290	(\ref{sfcA4}) implies $\wkl$, let $f$ and $g$ be injections with disjoint ranges.  Construct $H$ as follows.
   291	The vertices of $H$ are $V = \{ v_i \mid i \in \nat \}$.  Using a bijective pair encoding, for each $i \in \nat$,
   292	let $i_0$ and $i_1$ denote the components of the pair encoded by $i$.  For each $i$, define the edge
   293	$e_i = \{ v_{f(i_0 )} , v_{g(i_1 )} \}$.  Let $E_0$ be any finite set of edges with indices bounded by $b$, and
   294	let $V_0$ be some set of vertices containing all the vertices in edges in $E_0$.  The coloring which assigns
   295	color 0 to all vertices of the form $v_{f(t)}$ for $t<b$ and color 1 to all other vertices of $V_0$ is a strong
   296	$2$-coloring of the finite partial hypergraph defined by $E_0$ and $V_0$.  By (\ref{sfcA4}), $H$ has a strong
   297	$k$-coloring for some $k$;  call it $\chi$.  By bounded $\Sigma_1^0$ comprehension, provable in $\rca$ by Exercise II.3.13 of
   298	Simpson \cite{simpson}, the set $K = \{ j<k \mid \exists t ~ \chi( v_{f(t) }) = j\}$ exists.  By recursive comprehension,
   299	the set $S = \{ n \mid \chi (v_n ) \in K \}$ exists.  By the construction of $H$, $S$ contains all elements of the range
   300	of $f$ and excludes all elements of the range of $g$.
   301	\end{proof}
   302	
   303	In the preceding theorem, if the number 3 is replaced by 2 in item (\ref{sfcA3}), the resulting statement asserts
   304	that every locally $2$-colorable graph has a finite coloring.  This statement is known to be equivalent to $\wkl$
   305	over $\rca$ plus $\Sigma^0_2$ induction, but the equivalence over $\rca$ is one of many open questions
   306	listed in section \S5 of Dorais, Hirst, and Shafer \cite{dhs}.  Item (\ref{sfcA4}) shows
   307	that for graphs with edges presented as a sequence rather than as a set, the statement that every locally $2$-colorable
   308	graph has a finite coloring is equivalent to $\wkl$ over $\rca$.
   309	
   310	
   311	\subsection*{Vertex colorings and infinite edges}
   312	
   313	Allowing hypergraphs with infinite edges significantly affects the nature of vertex colorings.  Clearly, any
   314	strong vertex coloring of a hypergraph with an infinite edge must use infinitely many colors.  Hypergraphs
   315	with infinite edges may or may not have finite proper or conflict-free colorings.  We will show that sorting
   316	those graphs with finite colorings from those without requires $\poo$.  The next definition and theorem assist
   317	in that proof.
   318	
   319	\begin{defn}
   320	If $T$ is a tree, the set $L$ of {\sl leaves of }$T$ consists of those sequences in $T$ which have no extensions.
   321	That is, $L = \{ \sigma \in T \mid \forall n ~\sigma \cat n \notin T\}$.
   322	\end{defn}
   323	
   324	\begin{lemma}\label{leaves}
   325	$(\rca )$  The follow are equivalent.
   326	\begin{enumerate}
   327	\item $\poo$.\label{leaves1}
   328	\item  If $\seq T$ is a sequence of trees in $\nat^{<\nat}$, then there is a function $f: \nat \to 2$ such that
   329	$f(i) = 1$ if and only if $T_i$ contains an infinite path.\label{leaves2}
   330	\item  If $\seq T$ is a sequence of trees and $\seq L$ is a sequence of sets such that for each $i$, $L_i$ is the set
   331	of leaves of $T_i$, then there is a function $f: \nat \to 2$ such that
   332	$f(i) = 1$ if and only if $T_i$ contains an infinite path.\label{leaves3}
   333	\end{enumerate}
   334	\end{lemma}
   335	
   336	\begin{proof}
   337	We work in $\rca$.  The equivalence of (\ref{leaves1}) and (\ref{leaves2}) is Lemma VI.1.1 of Simpson \cite{simpson}.
   338	Because (\ref{leaves3}) is a special case of (\ref{leaves2}), we need only prove that (\ref{leaves3}) implies (\ref{leaves2}).
   339	
   340	Let $\seq T$ be a sequence of trees as in (\ref{leaves2}).  For any sequence $\sigma$ of positive integers, let
   341	$\sigma_{-1}$ denote the sequence of the same length as $\sigma$ such that for all $i$, $\sigma_{-1}(i) = \sigma (i)-1$.
   342	For each $i$, define the tree ${\hat T}_i$ by letting $\tau \in {\hat T}_i$ if and only if either $\tau_{-1} \in T_i$ or
   343	$\tau = \sigma\cat 0$ and $\sigma_{-1} \in T_i$.  For each $i$, the leaf set of $\hat T_i$ consists precisely of those
   344	sequences of the form $\sigma_{-1} \cat 0$ such that $\sigma \in T_i$.  $\rca$ can prove that $\seq{\hat T}$
   345	and $\seq L$ exist.  Note that $p$ is an infinite path in $\hat T_i$ if and only if $p_{-1}$ is an infinite path in $T$.
   346	Thus, the function $f$ satisfying (\ref{leaves3}) for $\seq{\hat T}$ also satisfies (\ref{leaves2}) for $\seq T$.
   347	\end{proof}
   348	
   349	The preceding result allows us to prove the reversal below in a single step, rather than using (\ref{p2}) to prove
   350	$\aca$ and then deducing $\poo$ in a second step.
   351	
   352	\begin{thm}
   353	$(\rca )$  For each $k \ge 2$, the following are equivalent.
   354	\begin{enumerate}
   355	\item  $\poo$.\label{p1}
   356	\item  If $\seq H$ is a sequence of hypergraphs, then there is a function $f: \nat \to 2$ such that
   357	$f(i) = 1$ if and only if $H_i$ has a proper $k$-coloring.\label{p2}
   358	\item  Statement $(\ref{p2})$ with ``proper'' replaced by ``conflict-free.''\label{p3}
   359	\end{enumerate}
   360	\end{thm}
   361	
   362	\begin{proof}
   363	Assume $\rca$.  To prove that (\ref{p1}) implies (\ref{p2}), fix $k \ge 2$ and suppose
   364	$\seq H$ is a sequence of hypergraphs.  Assuming that the vertices of $H$ are a subset of $\nat$,
   365	the statement ``$H$ has a proper $k$-coloring'' asserts the existence of a function $g:\nat \to k$ such that
   366	$g$ is not constant on any edge.  Thus, the set of indices $i$ such that $H_i$ has a proper $k$-coloring
   367	is definable by a $\Sigma^1_1$ formula.  Applying $\poo$, the complement of this set of indices
   368	exists, so by recursive comprehension, the set of indices and its characteristic function also exist.  This
   369	function satisfies item (\ref{p2}) of the theorem.  A similar argument shows that (\ref{p1}) implies (\ref{p3}).
   370	
   371	Next, we will prove that (\ref{p2}) implies (\ref{p1}) for the case $k=2$, indicating parenthetically how to
   372	modify the argument for (\ref{p3}) implies (\ref{p1}).  By Lemma \ref{leaves}, it suffices to use (\ref{p2})
   373	to determine which trees are well-founded in a list of trees with leaf sets.  Let $\seq{T}$ be a sequence of trees
   374	in $\nat ^{<\nat}$, and let $\seq{L}$ be the corresponding leaf sets.  Given any tree
   375	$T \subset \nat^{\nat}$ with leaf set $L$, define a hypergraph $H$ as follows.
   376	The vertices of $H$ are $\{ a_0 , a_1, b_0, b_1 , s\}$ together with vertices labeled
   377	$\sigma_0$ and $\sigma_1$ for each nonempty sequence $\sigma$ in $T$.  (One can routinely
   378	assign integer codes to these vertices, and arrange for the set of codes to be $\nat$ or an initial segment of $\nat$,
   379	if so desired.)
   380	The edges of $H$ consist of
   381	\begin{list}{$\bullet$}{}
   382	\item  $(a_0, a_1 )$, $(a_1, s)$, $(b_0 ,b_1 )$, and $(b_1 , s)$,
   383	\item  $(\sigma_0 , \sigma_1 )$ for every nonempty $\sigma \in T$,
   384	\item  $(\sigma_1 , s)$ if $\sigma$ is a leaf of $T$,
   385	\item $E_\sigma = \{ \sigma_1 \}\cup\{ \tau_0  \mid \tau\in T \land \exists n ~ \tau = \sigma\cat n\}$ if $\sigma\in T$ is not a leaf, and
   386	\item $E_0 = \{a_0, b_0\} \cup \{ \sigma_0 \mid \sigma\in T \land \lh (\sigma ) = 1\}$.
   387	\end{list}
   388	Using $T$ and $L$ as parameters, $\rca$ can prove the existence of $H$ uniformly.
   389	Note that $E_0$ and $E_\sigma$ may be infinite edges.
   390	
   391	Suppose $c: \nat \to \{ \mred,~ \mblue \}$ is any $2$-coloring of the vertices of $H$.
   392	Swapping colors if necessary, let $s$ be blue.  Assume $c$ is proper.  Then $a_1$ and $b_1$ are red
   393	and $a_0$ and $b_0$ are blue.  Also, for any $\sigma \in T$ if $\sigma_0$ is red, then $\sigma_1$ is blue,
   394	so $\{ \sigma_1 ,s \}$ is not an edge and $\sigma$ is not a leaf.  Because $\sigma_1$ is blue, by the definition
   395	of $E_\sigma$, for some immediate successor $\tau$ of $\sigma$, $\tau_0$ must be red.  Finally,
   396	to properly color $E_0$, for some $\sigma \in T$ of length 1, $\sigma_0$ is red.  Let $n$ be the least value such
   397	that for $\tau = \sigma\cat n$, $\tau_0$ is red.  Iterating this process traces an infinite path in $T$.  Summarizing,
   398	if $c$ properly $2$-colors $H$, then $T$ has an infinite path.  (Every conflict-free coloring is proper, so if $c$ is
   399	conflict-free then $T$ has an infinite path.)
   400	
   401	Conversely, suppose that $T$ has an infinite path $p$, and let $\sigma^0 \subset \sigma^1 \subset \sigma^2 \subset \dots$ be
   402	the nonempty initial segments of $p$.  Define $c$ by $c(s)=c(a_0) = c(b_0) = \mblue$,
   403	$c(a_1)=c(b_1)=\mred$, $\sigma^i_0 = \mred$ and $\sigma^1_1 = \mblue$ for $\sigma^i$ in $p$, and
   404	$\tau_0 = \mblue$ and $\tau_1 = \mred$ for $\tau\in T$ not in $p$.  Treating the definitions of the edges of $H$ as
   405	cases, one can verify that $c$ is a proper (conflict-free) $2$ coloring of $H$.  Summarizing the last two paragraphs,
   406	$H$ has a proper (conflict-free) $2$-coloring if and only if $T$ has an infinite path.
   407	
   408	Carrying out the the construction uniformly for all the trees in $\seq{T}$ and applying (\ref{p2})
   409	of the theorem, we can find the characteristic function for the well-founded trees, as desired.
   410	The parenthetical comments show that for $k=2$, (\ref{p3}) implies (\ref{p1}).
   411	
   412	For values of $k>2$, modify the construction of the previous reversal by adding a complete graph
   413	with $k-2$ vertices to $H$, connecting each vertex of the complete graph to every vertex of $H$ by
   414	an edge consisting of two vertices.  The resulting hypergraph has a proper (conflict-free) $k$-coloring if and only
   415	if $T$ is not well-founded.
   416	\end{proof}
   417	
   418	It would be interesting to know if the preceding result continues to hold if ``$k$-coloring'' is replaced by
   419	``finite coloring.''
   420	Lemma \ref{leaves} may prove useful in recasting the preceding theorem as a result related
   421	to $\Sigma^1_1$ completness using many-one reducibility, or as a result on Weihrauch reducibility.
   422	
   423	
   424	
   425	
   426	Infinite edges can interfere with finite conflict-free colorings of hypergraphs.  The following graph illustrates this situation.
   427	
   428	\begin{defn}\label{Mdefn}
   429	The $\cal M$-graph (the Matryoshka graph) is the hypergraph with vertex set $\nat$ and edges $\{ E_j \mid j\in \nat\}$
   430	where $E_j = \{ k \mid j\le k\}$.
   431	\end{defn}
   432	
   433	Every finite partial subhypergraph of the $\cal M$-graph has a conflict-free 2-coloring.  For example, given any finite
   434	collection of vertices, simply color the largest numbered vertex red and all other vertices blue.  On the other hand,
   435	the entire $\cal M$-graph has no finite conflict-free coloring.  This can be proved directly by induction on $\Sigma^0_2$
   436	formulas, or by using the following lemma.
   437	
   438	\begin{lemma}$(\rca )$\label{ert}
   439	The following are equivalent.
   440	\begin{enumerate}
   441	\item  $\sf{ERT}$ (eventually repeating tails):  Suppose $f : \nat \to k$ for some $k \in \nat$.\label{ert1}
   442	Then there is a $b \in \nat $ such that  for all $ x \ge b $ there is a $y \ge b$ such that $ x \neq y$ and $f(x) = f(y)$.
   443	\item  No finite coloring of the $\cal M$-graph is conflict-free.\label{ert2}
   444	\end{enumerate}
   445	\end{lemma}
   446	
   447	\begin{proof}
   448	We will work in $\rca$.  To prove that (\ref{ert1}) implies (\ref{ert2}), suppose that $f: \nat \to k$ is a finite coloring
   449	of the $\cal M$-graph.   Apply $\sf{ERT}$ to find $b$ such that for all $x\ge b$ there is a $y \ge b$ such that $y \neq x$
   450	and $f(x) = f(y)$.  Then every color appearing in $E_b$ appears at least twice.  Thus $f$ is not conflict-free.
   451	
   452	To prove the converse, let $f : \nat \to k$ be any function.  We can view $f$ as a $k$-coloring of the $\cal M$-graph.
   453	By (\ref{ert2}), $f$ is not conflict-free.  Thus there is an edge $E_b$ such that every color appearing in $b$ appears at
   454	least twice.  Thus $b$ witnesses that $\sf {ERT}$ holds.
   455	\end{proof}
   456	
   457	The principle $\sf{ERT}$ follows trivially from the principle $\sf{ECT}$ which asserts that if $f: \nat \to k$ then
   458	there is a $b$ such that for all $x \ge b$ there are infinitely many values of $y$ such that $f(x) = f(y)$.  By
   459	Theorem 6 of Hirst \cite{ind}, $\sf{ECT}$ is equivalent to $\Sigma_2^0$ induction.  Thus, ${\sf{I}} \Sigma^0_2$ suffices
   460	to prove that no finite coloring of the $\cal M$-graph is conflict-free.
   461	
   462	We will show that this use of induction is not necessary by proving $\sf{ERT}$ from $\srt$, the stable Ramsey
   463	theorem for pairs and two colors.
   464	By Corollary 2.6 of Chong, Slaman, and Yang \cite{csy}, $\srt$ cannot prove ${\sf{I}}\Sigma^0_2$, so neither can
   465	$\sf{ERT}$.
   466	
   467	\begin{thm}\label{srtert}
   468	$(\rca)$ $\srt$ implies $\sf{ERT}$.
   469	\end{thm}
   470	
   471	\begin{proof}
   472	Let $f : \nat \to k$.  Define $g:[\nat ]^2 \to 2 $ by $g(a,b) = 1$ if and only if for some $x \in [a,b)$,
   473	$f(x)$ appears exactly once in the range of $f$ restricted to $[a,b)$.  Because the range of $f$ is $k$,
   474	for fixed $a$ and increasing $x$, the value of $g(a,x)$ can only change at most $2k$ times.  Thus $g$ is a
   475	stable coloring.
   476	
   477	Apply $\srt$ to $g$ to obtain an infinite set $H= \{ x_0 , x_1 , x_2 \dots \}$ that is monochromatic for $g$.  Suppose,
   478	by way of contradiction, that $g([H ]^2 ) \equiv 1$.  Let $H_0$ consist of the first $3 \cdot 2^{k-1} $ elements
   479	of $H$.  The elements of $H_0$ define $3\cdot 2^{k-1} -1$ consecutive half-open intervals. Because
   480	$g(x_0, x_{3\cdot 2^{k-1} } ) = 1$, some value of $f$ appears exactly once in $[x_0 , x_{3\cdot 2^{k-1}})$.  Call this
   481	$k_0$.  Either on the left or the right of this location, there must be a collection of $3\cdot 2^{k-2}$ consecutive
   482	elements of $H_0$.  Call these $H_1$.  Note that the range of $f$ on the intervals defined by $H_1$ must omit $k_0$.
   483	Thus, the range of $f$ on these intervals contains at most $k-1$ elements.  Iterating this construction,
   484	$H_{k-1}$ will consist of three consecutive elements of $H_0$ such that $f$ is constant on the union of the two associated
   485	subintervals.  No value of $f$ appears exactly once in the range of $f$ on this union, contradicting the assumption
   486	that $g$ applied to the endpoints yields $1$.
   487	Thus $g([H]^2 ) \equiv 0$ and every value of $f$ appearing at or after $x_0$ must appear at least twice.
   488	\end{proof}
   489	
   490	It would be interesting to know how $\sf{ERT}$ compares in strength to other statements that are weaker
   491	than ${\sf I}\Sigma^0_2$, for example, like those described by
   492	Kreuzer and Yokoyama
   493	\cite{ky}
