codex
The paper’s main finite-coloring results are logical compactness/reversal theorems, not finite-instance complexity theorems. Its exact definitions matter: “proper” means nonconstant on every edge of size \(>1\), “strong” means injective on every edge, and “conflict-free” means every edge has a uniquely occurring color. I’m now checking whether the proof constructions yield a legitimate Track B compression gap; a generic planted finite coloring would not inherit hardness from these reverse-mathematics equivalences.
